course: dictionary
lesson: 1896
title: Colonise and its two meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: To colonise means that a country sends people to settle in a place and takes control of the land. Look at the clues in this sentence: «Thailand, with its capital Bangkok, is the only country in South-East Asia never colonised by a European power.» Thailand is the exception, so the other countries nearby must have been taken over by European powers. Colonised means taken over.
explain: The dictionary adds a clue: the land was often taken «from the people already living there». Many lands were called empty by the newcomers, but «nearly all of them were home to people with their own nations and laws». So colonise is more than moving somewhere. It means taking control, and it often harmed the people who were already there.
explain: Scientists use the same word in a different way. «Moss colonises bare rock» means moss spreads into a bare place and starts living and growing there. No country or people are taken over here. The clue is that living things, such as moss or bacteria, simply spread into a new place.
explain: Look at the word family: colony is a place that has been colonised, a colonist is a settler, colonial means about colonies, and decolonise means to free a country from control. Colonise is a verb, so it changes: colonises, colonised, colonising. In US spelling it is colonize.
fact: Colonise means to send people to settle a place and take control of it.
fact: «Thailand ... was never colonised by a European power» because it kept its freedom by clever diplomacy, which means careful talks and deals.
fact: In science, «colonise» means living things spread into a new place and start living and growing there.
fact: «Moss colonises bare rock» and «bacteria colonise a wound» are two science examples.
fact: Colony, colonist, colonial, colonisation and decolonise all come from the same root word, so learning one helps you understand the others.
exercise: «Thailand was never colonised by a European power.» What does colonised mean here? | Given a gift of food and water | Made into a large, busy city | *Taken over and controlled by people from another country | Painted in bright colours
exercise: A scientist says «bacteria colonise a wound». What does it mean? | They heal the wound with medicine | *They spread into the wound and start living and growing there | They leave the wound and go away
exercise: Which word means a settler who goes to live in a colony? | *colonist | colony | colonise | decolonise
test: «EU5 shows many lands as empty and waiting to be colonised, but nearly all of them were home to people with their own nations and laws.» What clue shows that colonise can mean taking over land that already had people on it? | The lands were empty | Games are fun to play | It is a long word | *The lands were home to people with their own nations and laws
test: Which word belongs to the same family as colonise? | *colony | cosy | colourful | castle
exam: In which sentence does colonise mean living things spreading into a new place? | «Bangkok is the capital of Thailand.» | «Thailand was never colonised by a European power.» | *«Moss colonises bare rock.» | «The lands were home to people with their own laws.»
